Adversarial review (thanks) found the badges/doc OVERCLAIMED enforcement. An
audit of every consumer of the resolved graph confirmed: nothing denies on
workflow.gates.<id>.enabled today. B6 exists to kill exactly this mis-sell, so
the vocabulary now matches TODAY's wiring (no new enforcement added).

- Stage-exit gates + rails: had ZERO runtime deny consumer (enforce-stage
  enforces order+completion, independent of these flags). Relabel
  "ENFORCED (gate/rail)" -> "DECLARED (no runtime consumer yet)", locus
  "registry — declared, not yet enforced".
- gate.issue_verify: consumed by _issue.js but WARN-ONLY -> "VERIFY (warn-only)".
- Human gates: approve/check is real + deny-capable but no chokepoint invokes it
  -> "DENY-ON-CHECK", locus "deny-on-check (no chokepoint yet)".
- ENFORCED reserved strictly for a wired runtime deny (empty set today);
  badgeFor never emits it. Tests assert the honesty invariant (no badge ENFORCED)
  and the flagship "resolver honors it" tests reframed as registry REFLECTION,
  not denial.
- Guarantee doc: honest headline (real enforcement lives in B3 lefthook, B2
  fail-closed validate/preflight, enforce-stage order+completion — all
  independent of these flags). Removed the enforce-stage/B2 citation from the
  gate row (that behavior does not depend on workflow.gates.<id>.enabled).

Clean things kept clean: refusals, write mechanics, locked protection, advisory
never controllable. Wiring the registry to real enforcement = separate post-beta
issue (per review), NOT attempted here.

Issues: 7dc59af2, 724356ea
